# 🚀 Pre-Deployment Review — Crammerly

Comprehensive audit across security, infrastructure, performance, and code quality.

---

## 🔴 CRITICAL — Fix Before Deploy

> [!CAUTION]
> These issues could cause security breaches or data exposure in production.

### 1. [server_log.txt](file:///c:/Users/Bushra/shabucks/backend/server_log.txt) is tracked in Git (503 KB)
[backend/server_log.txt](file:///c:/Users/Bushra/shabucks/backend/server_log.txt) is committed to the repository. It may contain sensitive request data, IP addresses, and stack traces.

**Fix:** Remove from git tracking and add to [.gitignore](file:///c:/Users/Bushra/shabucks/.gitignore):
```bash
git rm --cached backend/server_log.txt
echo "backend/server_log.txt" >> .gitignore
```

---

### 2. Supabase key exposed in [frontend/.env](file:///c:/Users/Bushra/shabucks/frontend/.env)
The file contains a Supabase anon key (`VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJ...`). While anon keys are designed to be public, **this file is [.gitignore](file:///c:/Users/Bushra/shabucks/.gitignore)d but still in your working directory**. Verify:
- Is Supabase actively used? It's not referenced anywhere in the current frontend code.
- If not used, **remove these lines** to avoid confusion.
- If used, ensure Row Level Security (RLS) is enabled on all Supabase tables.

---

### 3. Backend [.env.production](file:///c:/Users/Bushra/shabucks/backend/.env.production) has placeholder credentials
The file contains `MONGO_URI=mongodb+srv://admin:change-me@cluster0.example...` and `JWT_SECRET=production_secret_at_least_64_characters...`. These are clearly placeholders, but verify:
- **Before deploying**, replace with real secrets via your deployment platform's secret management (Render, Railway, etc.)
- **Never** put real secrets in this file — use environment variables from your hosting platform

---

### 4. CSP allows `'unsafe-inline'` for scripts
In [app.js](file:///c:/Users/Bushra/shabucks/backend/app.js) (line 102):
```js
scriptSrc: ["'self'", "'unsafe-inline'"]
```
This weakens XSS protection significantly. If you need inline scripts (e.g., Google OAuth), use nonces instead:
```js
scriptSrc: ["'self'", `'nonce-${req.nonce}'`]
```
If no inline scripts are needed, remove `'unsafe-inline'` entirely.

---

### 5. MongoDB & Redis ports exposed in Docker
In [docker-compose.yml](file:///c:/Users/Bushra/shabucks/docker-compose.yml), MongoDB (27017) and Redis (6379) are exposed to the host. In production, these should only be accessible internally:
```diff
  mongodb:
-   ports:
-     - "27017:27017"
+   # No ports exposed — only accessible via internal Docker network
    
  redis:
-   ports:
-     - "6379:6379"
+   # No ports exposed — only accessible via internal Docker network
```

---

### 6. Nginx has no HTTPS configuration
[nginx/default.conf](file:///c:/Users/Bushra/shabucks/nginx/default.conf) only listens on port 80. For production:
- Add SSL certificate configuration (Let's Encrypt via certbot)
- Redirect HTTP → HTTPS
- The docker-compose exposes port 443 but nginx doesn't handle it

---

## 🟡 IMPORTANT — Should Fix

### 7. No `X-Frame-Options` conflict check
Both Helmet (`frameguard: "deny"`) and nginx (`X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN`) set `X-Frame-Options` to different values. The browser will see **two conflicting headers**. Remove the nginx one since Helmet handles it consistently.

---

### 8. CSP `imgSrc` is restrictive
Current CSP only allows images from `'self'`, `data:`, and `sentry.io`. But the app loads images from:
- `images.unsplash.com` (blog posts)
- User avatars (potentially external URLs)

**Fix:** Add allowed image origins:
```js
imgSrc: ["'self'", "data:", "https://*.sentry.io", "https://images.unsplash.com", "https://*.googleusercontent.com"]
```

---

### 9. Missing `PATCH` in CORS methods
CORS allows `['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE']` but **not `PATCH`**. If any future endpoint uses PATCH, it will be silently blocked.

---

### 10. No production build test
The frontend [vite.config.js](file:///c:/Users/Bushra/shabucks/frontend/vite.config.js) disables sourcemaps in production (`sourcemap: false`) which is correct, but you should run `npm run build` and verify the production bundle before deploying to catch any build-time errors.

---

### 11. Stale test files in backend
[backend/test_api.js](file:///c:/Users/Bushra/shabucks/backend/test_api.js) and [backend/test_api_2.js](file:///c:/Users/Bushra/shabucks/backend/test_api_2.js) appear to be ad-hoc test scripts. These should either:
- Be moved to `backend/tests/`
- Or deleted before deployment

---

### 12. `server_name localhost` in nginx
The nginx config uses `server_name localhost` instead of your actual domain. For production, change to your real domain:
```diff
-server_name localhost;
+server_name crammerly.io www.crammerly.io;
```

---

## 🟢 NICE-TO-HAVE

### 13. Missing favicon/meta for production
Verify `frontend/index.html` has:
- Proper `<title>` and `<meta description>`
- Open Graph tags for social sharing
- A proper favicon (not the default Vite icon)

---

### 14. No request timeout on Axios client
The frontend API client has no explicit timeout. Add a default:
```js
const client = axios.create({
    baseURL: env.apiUrl,
    timeout: 15000, // 15 second timeout
    // ...
});
```

---

### 15. Healthcheck endpoint returns data before `responseEnhancer`
The `/health` route uses `res.sendSuccess()` which comes from the `responseEnhancer` middleware. This works, but the `${API_PREFIX}/health` detailed endpoint could leak diagnostic info (memory, uptime) if not protected. Consider adding `protect` middleware or restricting to internal IPs.

---

### 16. Docker healthcheck uses `fetch` instead of `curl`
The Dockerfile healthcheck uses `node -e "fetch(...)..."` which loads the full Node runtime for each check. Use `wget` or `curl` instead:
```dockerfile
HEALTHCHECK CMD wget --no-verbose --tries=1 --spider http://localhost:5000/health || exit 1
```
Note: `curl` isn't available in Alpine by default, but `wget` is.

